readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Vanbrugh'', Love Disarmed.}}}}
'''untappice†''' (un-tap′is), ''v.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''tappice'', ''tappish''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To come out of concealment.
{{smaller block|Now I’ll ''untappice.''
{{right|''Fletcher and Massinger'', A Very Woman, iii. 5.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To drive out of concealment, as game.

'''untarnished''' (un-tär′nisht), ''a.'' Not soiled; not tarnished; not stained; unblemished: as, ''untarnished'' silk; an ''untarnished'' reputation.

'''untaste†''' (un-tāst′), ''v. t.'' To take away a taste from; cause to feel disgust or distaste for.
{{smaller block|<poem>Could not by all means might be devis’d
''Untaste'' them of this great disgust.</poem>
{{right|''Daniel'', Civil Wars, viii.}}}}

'''untasted''' (un-tās′ted), ''a.'' Not tasted; not tried by the taste or tongue; hence, not experienced or enjoyed.

'''untaught''' (un-tât′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''untaught'', ''untaght''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''taught''<sup>1</sup>.] Not taught. {{smaller|(''a'') Not instructed; not educated; unlettered; illiterate.}}
{{smaller block|Better unfedde then ''vn-taughte.''
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 348.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Unskilled; not having use or practice.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Suffolk’s imperial tongue is stern and rough,
. . . ''untaught'' to plead for favour.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iv. 1. 122.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Not made the subject of teaching or instruction; not communicated by teaching.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>With ''untaught'' Joy Pharaoh the News does hear,
And little thinks their Fate attends on him, and his so near.</poem>
{{right|''Cowley'', Pindaric Odes, xiv. 12.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller|(''d'') Not having learned by experience; ignorant.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Insatiate to pursue
Vain war with heaven; and, by success ''untaught'',
His proud imaginations thus displayed.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ii. 9.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Untaught'' that soon such anguish must ensue.
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Female Vagrant.}}}}

'''untax''' (un-taks′), ''v. t.'' To remove a tax from.
{{smaller block|''Untax'' the clothing of sixty million people.
{{right|''Report of Sec. of Treasury'', 1886, I. lvii.}}}}

'''untaxed''' (un-takst′), ''a.'' Not taxed. {{smaller|(''a'') Not charged with or liable to pay taxes. ''T. Warton.'' (''b'') Not charged with any fault, offense, etc.; not accused.}}
{{smaller block|Common speech, which leaves no virtue ''untaxed''.
{{right|''Bacon'', Learning, i.}}}}

'''unteach''' (un-tēch′), ''v. t.'' '''1.''' To cause to forget, disbelieve, or give up what has been taught.
{{smaller block|If they chanc’t to be taught any thing good, or of their own accord had learn’t it, they might see that presently ''untaught'' them by the custome and ill example of their elders. {{right|''Milton'', Apology for Smectymnuus.}}}}
'''2.''' To make forgotten; make to cease from being acquired by instruction.
{{smaller block|But we, by art, ''unteach'' what nature taught.
{{right|''Dryden'', Indian Emperour, i. 1.}}}}

'''unteachable''' (un-tē′cha-bl), ''a.'' Not teachable or docile; indocile. ''Milton'', Tetrachordon.

'''unteachableness''' (un-tē′cha-bl-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being unteachable; absence of docility.

'''unteam''' (un-tēm′), ''v. t.'' To unyoke a team from; take a team, as of horses or oxen, from.
{{smaller block|Justice and authority laid by the rods and axes as soon as the sun ''unteamed'' his chariot.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'' (ed. 1835), Works, I. 212.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
'''untell''' (un-tel′), ''v. t.'' To recall, as what has been told; make as if not told or enumerated.
{{smaller block|That time could turne up his swift sandy glasse<br>To ''untell'' the dayes, and to redeeme these hours.
{{right|''Heywood'', Woman Killed with Kindness.}}}}

'''untemper''' (un-tem′pėr), ''v. t.'' To remove the temper from, as metal; hence, to soften; mollify.
{{smaller block|I dare not swear thou lovest me; yet my blood begins to flatter me that thou dost, notwithstanding the poor and ''untempering'' effect of my visage.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., v. 2. 241.}}
The study of sciences does more soften and ''untemper'' the courages of men than any way fortifie and incite them. ''Cotton'', tr. of Montaigne’s Essays, xix. (''Davies.'')}}

'''untemperate†''' (un-tem′pėr-āt), ''a.'' Intemperate. ''Times’ Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 58.

'''untemperately†''' (un-tem′pėr-ạ̄t-li), ''adv.'' Intemperately.

'''untempered''' (un-tem′pėrd), ''a.'' Not tempered. {{smaller|(''a'') Not duly mixed for use: as, ''untempered'' lime.}}
{{smaller block|So it was not long that this ''vntempered'' mortar would hold together these buildings.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 273.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Not brought to the desired state of hardness: as, ''untempered'' steel. (''c'') Not brought to a fit or proper state generally; not regulated, moderated, or controlled; not mollified: as, ''untempered'' severity. ''Johnson'', Life of Waller.}}
{{smaller block|The ''untempered'' spirit of madness.
{{right|''Burke'', Appeal from Old to New Whigs.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
used only in the phrases ''his'', ''thy'', etc., ''unthankes'', not of his, thy, etc., accord; involuntarily.

¶
'''unthankful''' (un-thangk′fu̇l), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not thankful; ungrateful; not making acknowledgments for good received. Luke vi. 35.—'''2.''' Not repaid with thanks; unacceptable.
{{smaller block|One of the most ''unthankful'' offices in the world.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', The Bee, No. 8.}}}}
'''3.''' Giving no return; unproductive.
{{smaller block|The husbandman ought not, for one ''unthankful'' year, to forsake the plough. ''B. Jonson'', Bartholomew Fair, iii. 1.}}

'''unthankfully''' (un-thangk′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' In an unthankful or ungrateful manner; without thanks; ungratefully. ''Boyle.''

'''unthankfulness''' (un-thangk′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' Ungratefulness; want of a sense of kindness or benefits; ingratitude.
{{smaller block|Immoderate favours breed first ''unthankfulness'', and afterward hate.
{{right|''Sir J. Hayward.''}}}}

'''unthink''' (un-thingk′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''think''.] To retract in thought; remove from the mind or thought; think differently about.
{{smaller block|<poem>To ''unthink'' your speaking,
And to say so no more.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., ii. 4. 104.}}
That the same thing is not thought and ''unthought'', resolved and unresolved, a thousand times in a day.
{{right|''J. Howe'', Works, I. 71.}}}}

'''unthinkability''' (un-thing-ka-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''unthinkable'' + ''-ity'' (see ''-bility'').] The character of being unthinkable.
{{smaller block|But genuine determinism occupies a totally different ground; not the impotence but the ''unthinkability'' of free-will is what it affirms.
{{right|''W. James'', Prin. of Psychol., II. 574.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|This kind of indifference or ''unthinkingness.''
{{right|''Lord Halifax.''}}}}

'''unthorny''' (un-thôr′ni), ''a.'' Not thorny; free from thorns. ''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., i. 5.

'''unthought''' (un-thât′), ''a.'' Not thought; not imagined or conceived; not considered: often followed by ''of'', formerly by ''on''.
{{smaller block|The ''unthought-on'' accident is guilty.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 4. 549.}}
<poem>This secure chapelry,
That had been offered to his doubtful choice
By an ''unthought-of'' patron.</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Excursion, vii.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To hold one unthought long†''', to hold one’s attention so as to keep one from wearying.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And I will go to jail-house door,
And ''hold the prisoner unthought lang.''</poem>
{{right|''Billie Archie'' (Child’s Ballads, VI. 95).}}
<poem>And ay as he harpit to the king,
To ''haud him unthought lang.''</poem>
{{right|''Glenkindie'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 8).}}}}

'''unthoughtfulness''' (un-thât′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being thoughtless; thoughtlessness.
{{smaller block|A constant æquable serenity and ''unthoughtfulness'' in outward accidents. {{right|''Bp. Fell'', Hammond, § 2.}}}}

'''unthread''' (un-thred′), ''v. t.'' '''1.''' To draw or take out a thread from: as, to ''unthread'' a needle.—'''2.''' To relax the ligaments of; loosen. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>He with his bare wand can ''unthread'' thy joints,
And crumble all thy sinews.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 614.}}}}
'''3.''' To find one’s way through.
{{smaller block|They soon ''unthreaded'' the labyrinth of rocks.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Spanish Nun, § 16.}}}}
